  "name": "Question 19",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13956996/assets/343897550f0f7b9e4a479e47c9191ababa31070dac79dbf4d54befe869661a48.png)\n\nGiven that the points $X$ and $Y$ with coordinates $(-1,2)$ and $(1,-2),$ respectively, are the endpoints of a diameter of a circle centered at the origin, what is the equation of the circle?",
  "worked_solution": "The equation of a circle with radius $r,$ centered at the origin, is \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the length of the diameter $\\overset{\u2015}{XY}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}XY & =\\sqrt{(-1-1)^{2}+(2-(-2))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+16} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\overset{\u2015}{XY}$ is a diameter, the radius is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{XY}{2}=\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{2}=\\sqrt{5}.\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the circle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={(\\sqrt{5})}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
